.TH NYX 1 "2025" "NYX Framework" "NYX Manual"
.SH NAME
nyx \- NYX Offensive Security Framework master shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nyx
.RI [ command
.RI [ args... ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nyx
is the master interactive shell for the NYX Offensive Security
Framework. It provides a persistent REPL with line editing, tab
completion, command history, and access to all framework functionality.
.PP
When run with no arguments,
.B nyx
enters the interactive shell. From the shell, you can enter tool
contexts (e.g. type
.B pingsweep
to get a pingsweep-specific prompt), run workflows, manage sessions,
and inspect the framework.
.PP
NYX tools are invoked in-process through a shared tool registry.
The workflow engine chains tools into directed acyclic graphs (DAGs)
with data flowing between steps via expression-based wiring.
.SH SHELL COMMANDS
.TP
.BI run " workflow.json" " [OPTIONS]"
Execute a workflow file. Streams tool output live in human mode.
Supports
.B --var KEY=VALUE
for variable overrides and
.B -J
for JSON output.
.TP
.B session list
List all sessions stored in
.IR ~/.nyx/sessions/ .
.TP
.BI "session show" " id" " [-J]"
Show details of a specific session. Use
.B -J
for JSON output.
.TP
.BI "session clean" " [--older-than DAYS] [--force]"
Remove session directories.
.TP
.B info
Display framework version, installed tools, and session directory.
.TP
.B version
Print the framework version.
.TP
.B help
List all available commands.
.TP
.I tool-name
Enter that tool's interactive context. The prompt changes to
.BR nyx:tool-name> .
Type
.B back
to return to the main shell.
.TP
.B exit / quit
Exit the shell.
.SH LINE EDITING
The shell uses linenoise for line editing. Arrow keys navigate
history (up/down) and move the cursor (left/right). Tab completes
commands, flags, interface names, and file paths contextually.
.SH TOOL CONTEXTS
Typing a registered tool name (e.g.
.BR pingsweep ,
.BR portscan )
enters that tool's interactive context. The tool's own commands
become available and the prompt reflects the context. Type
.B back
to return to the main shell.
.PP
If a tool requires elevated privileges and the current process
lacks them, a warning is displayed suggesting
.BR "sudo nyx" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the interactive shell:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo nyx
.RE
.PP
Enter a tool context and run a scan:
.PP
.RS
.B nyx> pingsweep
.br
.B nyx:pingsweep> scan -c 192.168.1.0/24
.br
.B nyx:pingsweep> back
.RE
.PP
Run a workflow:
.PP
.RS
.B nyx> run workflows/net-discovery.json
.RE
.PP
Override a workflow variable:
.PP
.RS
.B nyx> run workflows/net-discovery.json --var subnet=10.0.0.0/24
.RE
.PP
List sessions:
.PP
.RS
.B nyx> session list
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.nyx/nyx_history
Command history for the master shell.
.TP
.I ~/.nyx/sessions/
Session storage directory.
